toolUseResult.result:
Branch builds cleanly and the auth/contexts/cli test suites pass. This is a well-tested refactor collapsing the multi-context (kubectl-style) auth model into a single "current login" stored in the OS keychain, with a one-time migration off `contexts.json`. Findings below are ranked; the top two are the substantive ones.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "internal/entireclient/contexts/contexts.go",
    "line": 214,
    "summary": "Migration leaks non-current logins' long-lived refresh tokens and silently drops them when the legacy file has contexts but no current_context.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user upgrades with a legacy contexts.json whose current_context is empty (reachable on main: File.Delete clears current_context without promoting, and entiredb writes the shared file). readNoLock's `current == nil` branch does `os.Remove(legacyPath); return &File{}` — it never runs the keychain-cleanup loop that the current!=nil branch does. Result: every stored context's access AND refresh tokens stay in the OS keychain as orphaned, now-unreferenced secrets that no future `entire logout` can ever address/clean, and the user is silently logged out of still-valid sessions with no server-side revocation. TestLoadMigratesOnlyCurrentLegacyContext only covers the current!=nil path, so this leak is untested."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/entireclient/contexts/contexts.go",
    "line": 244,
    "summary": "Migration deletes contexts.json, which the package doc previously stated is shared with entiredb, destroying entiredb's stored context metadata.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user of both `entire` and entiredb has cluster logins recorded in the shared ~/.config/entire/contexts.json (old package doc: \"Both CLIs share the same file so a login from either is visible to the other\"). On the first post-upgrade `entire` invocation, readNoLock/writeNoLock unconditionally `os.Remove(legacyPath)` after migrating only entire's one current login. entiredb's other context entries (core URL, handle, keychain-slot pointers) are gone; its cluster tokens remain in the keychain but become unaddressable, so entiredb reads as logged out of those clusters."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/entireclient/contexts/contexts.go",
    "line": 190,
    "summary": "contexts.Load now performs an OS-keychain read on every call, replacing a cheap local file read on hot/auth paths.",
    "failure_scenario": "Load is called per operation by git-remote-entire (every fetch/push), the import/hook path (importLoggedIn, advertised as \"local-only ... safe on the import path\"), and every control-plane/cluster auth resolution. Each now issues a keychain round-trip (tokenstore.Get for the metadata item) in addition to the existing token read, adding keychain latency (and, on some macOS configs, access-prompt surface) to paths that previously only touched a plain file."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/entireclient/contexts/contexts.go",
    "line": 203,
    "summary": "Any non-ErrNotFound keychain error in readNoLock becomes fatal for all auth-touching commands, where a missing/unreadable legacy file previously degraded to logged-out.",
    "failure_scenario": "If the OS keychain is temporarily unavailable or the user denies access, `tokenstore.Get(loginMetadataService, ...)` returns a non-ErrNotFound error and readNoLock returns `read login metadata: ...`. Every caller of contexts.Load (status, control-plane resolution, cluster/git resolution) then hard-errors instead of resolving to \"not logged in\", turning a transient keychain condition into a broken CLI across unrelated commands."
  }
]
```
